{
  "thinking": "Separate customers may have run legitimate workloads. Check company registrations, payments, and job dates. Sustained load may be batch inference. Compare sampled GPU-to-GPU traffic and outbound traffic. A single training run may have been split across job labels or clusters. Check sealed job timelines against cross-cluster traffic and power spectra. The operator may have omitted GPUs or systems. Compare manufacturer inventory with the operator's list and reconcile site energy. Missing logs or late payments may be ordinary recordkeeping gaps. Compare escrow with current logs and check payment dates. Start with the independent inventory.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Competing explanations and checks: (1) One training run was split across labels or clusters. Check cross-cluster traffic and compare timing in the sealed logs. (2) Separate customers ran independent jobs. Check customer registrations and replies, bank payments, and traffic between clusters. (3) Sustained load came from batch inference or periodic cooling. Compare power spectra with interconnect traffic and live chip probes. (4) An unlisted cluster hosted training. Compare manufacturer inventory with the operator's system list and check for unexplained grid energy. (5) Lost logs or late payments explain record gaps. Compare scheduler records with escrow and check payment timing against bank records and customer replies. Start with independent inventory to map the site.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Separate customers may have run independent training jobs below 50,000 GPU-hours each. Check customer identities and replies, escrowed schedules, and traffic between clusters. Sustained load may come from batch inference or periodic cooling. Compare grid power, outbound traffic, and power spectra with GPU-link traffic and live chip readings where available. Missing logs or late payments may explain record gaps. Compare the operator's logs with the sealed copies and check bank records and customer replies. One oversized run may have been split across labels, customers, or clusters. Look for cross-cluster traffic and matching activity, then use the fixed balance checks to assess GPU-hours. An oversized run may have used unlisted hardware or a low-traffic training setup. Compare manufacturer inventory with the operator's list, then check for unexplained grid load and use the available power and chip readings.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Competing explanations: (1) Legitimate batch inference ran at steady power. Check outbound traffic, interconnect traffic, and live chip probes where available. (2) Several independent customers trained below the threshold. Check sealed scheduler records against bank payments, customer registers, and traffic between clusters. (3) Missing logs or late payments made legitimate activity look unaccounted for. Compare escrow with the current logs, reconcile grid energy, and seek customer confirmation. (4) One coordinated run was split across labels or clusters. Check cross-cluster traffic and simultaneous activity, then estimate GPU-hours with the balance checks. (5) A run used unlisted hardware or unusually low interconnect traffic. Compare manufacturer inventory with the operator's list and check unexplained grid load against power spectra and available probes. Start with independent inventory to anchor the site map.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The manufacturer records show 2,000 GPUs delivered. Compare those shipments with the operator’s cluster, serial, and power-feed assignments to identify any missing or duplicate equipment and map the independent sensor checks.",
  "action": "system_list",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The manufacturer records 2,000 GPUs delivered to Eemshaven. Compare the operator’s cluster counts and serial ranges with those shipments to identify any hardware that needs independent follow-up.",
  "action": "system_list",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The listed clusters account for all 2,000 GPUs in the shipment records. Check the grid operator’s energy readings across all 30 days for sustained load or unexplained consumption. This will help identify periods for workload and coordination checks.",
  "action": "utility_energy",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The two listed clusters account for all 2,000 GPUs in the manufacturer’s shipment records. Get independent hourly energy data for all 30 days to identify sustained loads worth checking against the sealed scheduler records and interconnect measurements.",
  "action": "utility_energy",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The grid readings show three short load increases on different feeds. They could reflect separate jobs or stages of one coordinated run. Use the sealed scheduler records to identify job dates, node allocations, and customer labels, then test those records against independent measurements.",
  "action": "log_escrow",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "A steady 0.69 Hz line and inference-level traffic on Cluster A's listed links leave periodic batch inference and training on unlisted links as competing explanations. Read the sealed scheduler logs for the F1 plateau on days 12–19 and the smaller F2 spike on days 10–11. The records can show what the operator declared at the time and identify jobs to cross-check against independent evidence.",
  "action": "log_escrow",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The brief meter increases line up with the three logged jobs. The steady load could reflect ordinary inference or a sustained training run. Get the provider’s full-month traffic record to compare user demand with metered load and identify days for independent interconnect checks.",
  "action": "outbound_traffic",
  "args": {"first_day": 1, "last_day": 30}
}

{
  "thinking": "The three declared jobs total 24,000 GPU-hours, so relabeling or combining them alone cannot put a run over the threshold. The steady baseline could conceal additional training. Sample cluster A on day 20, when no scheduled job is declared, to test whether GPU-to-GPU traffic is consistent with inference or indicates undeclared training.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 20
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The outbound increase on days 14–15 supports Kestrel’s batch inference label. The steady load on other days could include unrecorded training. Run the fixed balance checks on the records gathered so far to identify discrepancies and guide independent interconnect sampling.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The balance checks support the declared workloads, but the two consecutive Cluster A jobs would total 50,400 GPU-hours if they were one run. Check Tidewater Robotics’ registration and ask whether its reply independently identifies the fine-tuning work it commissioned and the output it received.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Tidewater Robotics"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Tidewater’s registration and reply support its stated order. Check whether Ostara is independently registered and confirms commissioning and receiving its own fine-tuning output. The distinction matters because the two jobs would total 50,400 GPU-hours if they were phases of one run.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Ostara Legal"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Meridian’s registration and payment support a genuine customer relationship, but its silence leaves the workload unconfirmed. The periodic power signal fits both batch inference and training, and the tap sampled only listed links. Check provider records for a change in outbound traffic during days 12–19. Sustained egress aligned with the job would favor inference. Low egress would leave both explanations open because batch outputs can stay onsite.",
  "action": "outbound_traffic",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A’s day 14 traffic supports the batch inference explanation. On day 11, the grid measured a load increase on cluster B and the sealed log records a 150-GPU fine-tune. Sample B’s GPU-to-GPU traffic that day to test whether the workload fits the recorded fine-tune or points to a larger undeclared job.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "B",
    "day": 11
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Outbound traffic rose to 44.2 TB on the last day of Meridian’s job. That fits a bulk export of inference results, though the provider records show only site-wide totals. Run the fixed balance checks to reconcile both power increases with the declared jobs and identify any discrepancies that warrant independent follow-up.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster B’s day 11 traffic falls within the tap’s uncertainty for a 150-GPU fine-tune, and the grid’s 2.3 MWh increase fits that allocation. Check cluster A on day 4 to see whether its load increase fits the recorded 100-GPU fine-tune or suggests additional training.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 4
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "A single run could be split across labels or clusters, so check for traffic between clusters. Independent customer jobs could explain the logged bursts, so check customer registrations and replies. Batch inference or cooling could produce a periodic line, so corroborate any line with GPU-link traffic. Unlisted hardware could host training, so compare inventory with the system list and metered load. Missing logs or late payments could explain record gaps, so check escrow and bank records. The three internal-link samples found inference-level traffic on the sampled days. Switch to a power spectrum on F1 on day 20 to check for a training-like signal in the unsampled interval between days 12 and 27.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 20
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The grid readings and day 4 and day 11 taps fit the two recorded fine-tunes. Day 14 traffic supports batch inference, and the sealed copies recover the missing scheduler entries. A sustained undeclared run remains possible, including one using cross-cluster links outside the day 20 samples. The unpaid Lumen Health invoice could reflect a late payment or a misleading customer record. Check the bank records for payment history before testing Lumen’s identity and workload independently.",
  "action": "bank_payments",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster B’s day 11 traffic is consistent with the declared 150-GPU fine-tune within the tap’s uncertainty. Cluster A’s listed links showed inference-level traffic on days 13 and 15. Sample day 18 to check whether that pattern persisted late in the eight-day power plateau, before the outbound surge.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 18
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The day 20 cross-cluster tap found inference-level traffic, which weighs against coordination over the listed links that day. No independent GPU-link sample covers the first 11 days. Check Cluster A on day 7, when the meter returned to its usual level after the logged fine-tune, to test whether that steady load concealed training.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 7
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Lumen’s register entry and reply support ordinary interactive inference with an unpaid invoice. Separate fine-tunes and batch inference fit the sampled load spikes. The day 20 taps covered links within each cluster, so check the links between clusters for evidence of a coordinated run during the steady load.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A-B",
    "day": 20
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The consecutive Cluster A allocations would total 50,400 GPU-hours if they were one run. Two independently registered customers paid their respective invoices from their own accounts, and the power signature changed at the recorded handoff and remained near the new frequency on day 9. A single run could change its power signature when it moves to a new allocation, but the combined evidence makes separate runs at least four times as likely.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "NO_VIOLATION",
    "confidence": 88,
    "integrity": "CLEAN",
    "finding": "The evidence favors two separate 25,200 GPU-hour fine-tunes on Cluster A and supports no run above 50,000 GPU-hours.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "Tidewater Robotics, Ostara Legal, and Halcyon Bio each paid their respective invoices in full from accounts in their own names.",
        "source": "bank_payments",
        "vouched_by": "Bank for payments and account holders"
      },
      {
        "what": "The F1 power line shifted from 0.42 Hz on day 7 to 0.58 Hz on day 8 at the recorded handoff, then held near 0.57 Hz on day 9.",
        "source": "power_spectrum",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier's own sensor"
      },
      {
        "what": "Tidewater and Ostara have separate, longstanding registrations at their own addresses. Tidewater confirmed its order and allocation.",
        "source": "customer_check",
        "vouched_by": "Company register for registrations and Tidewater Robotics for its order"
      },
      {
        "what": "Cluster A carried about 397 GB/s of internal GPU traffic on day 6, consistent with training activity.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier's own optical tap"
      },
      {
        "what": "All 30 nightly scheduler copies were received and matched the presented log. Fixed checks found that metered energy matched declared jobs within 10% on all checked feed-days.",
        "source": "log_escrow and balance_checks",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier's archive and fixed program using grid records and declared allocations"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Compared the site list with manufacturer shipments and reviewed grid energy on all three feeds, site outbound traffic, and scheduler records for days 1–30. Direct workload measurements covered Cluster A on day 6 and feed F1 on days 7–9. Cluster B and cross-cluster links were untapped, no chips were probed, and checkpoint continuity was unverified.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The sustained load and flat outbound traffic fit the declared fine-tuning windows on both clusters. On Cluster A, separate customer payments and the power signature change favor a genuine handoff between the two adjacent allocations. Ostara's reply was unavailable within the audit window, but its registration and payment were independently verified. The later Cluster B load aligned with the declared Halcyon job and its invoice was paid from Halcyon's account."
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Three independent tap samples and the final-day outbound surge favor batch inference during the large Cluster A job. Training over unlisted links remains possible, but the combined evidence favors no violation by more than four to one.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "NO_VIOLATION",
    "confidence": 90,
    "integrity": "LIMITED",
    "finding": "No coordinated training run likely exceeded 50,000 GPU-hours. The 134,400 GPU-hour Cluster A job was likely batch inference.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "Cluster A's listed links carried about 8–13 kB/s per GPU on days 13, 15, and 18, consistent with inference and far below the low-communication training benchmark.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's optical tap"
      },
      {
        "what": "Site-wide outbound traffic rose from roughly 4.7–4.8 TB per day to 44.2 TB on the last day of the Cluster A job, consistent with a bulk results export.",
        "source": "outbound_traffic",
        "vouched_by": "internet provider"
      },
      {
        "what": "Daily metered energy matched declared jobs within 10% on all checked feed-days.",
        "source": "utility_energy and balance_checks",
        "vouched_by": "grid operator for meter readings and fixed program for reconciliation"
      },
      {
        "what": "Cluster B carried about 117 GB/s of internal GPU traffic on day 11, consistent within sampling uncertainty with the declared 150-GPU, 7,200 GPU-hour fine-tune.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap and scheduler_log",
        "vouched_by": "verifier's optical tap for traffic and operator for the job record"
      }
    ],
    "coverage": "Reviewed all 30 days of daily energy for F0–F2, site-wide outbound traffic, and scheduler records matched to escrow. The manufacturer shipment count matched the 2,000 listed GPUs. Sampled listed internal links on A on days 13, 15, and 18 and on B on day 11, and measured the F1 power spectrum on day 15. Cross-cluster and any unlisted links were unmeasured. Live chip probes could not cover the earlier jobs.",
    "anomalies_explained": "The F1 plateau and steady 0.69 Hz line are consistent with periodic batch inference given the three low-traffic samples. The early outbound shortfall and late excess fit deferred export, though the provider could not attribute the surge to Meridian. Meridian's registration and bank payment support a genuine customer relationship, but its silence left the workload unconfirmed. The F2 rise on days 10–11 fits the declared fine-tune, and the F0 rise coincided with the higher compute loads."
  }
}

